---
/*
 * Section 1 — the hero. Copy from the supplied design, on Fredrick's instruction of Aug 28
 * 2026: "design the page only with these contents".
 *
 * THIS REPLACES APPROVED COPY AND THAT WAS A DELIBERATE CALL, not a drift. The build
 * brief's approved hero was "Contact" / "Two ways to reach us." — a headline that only
 * made sense while the page offered two routes, a booking panel and a form. The design
 * removes the booking panel, so the page now offers one route and the old headline would
 * have been counting something that is no longer there.
 *
 * SINGLE COLUMN, and the right half stays empty. Every other hero on this site fills the
 * band — /about's runs two columns specifically because a left-aligned head in a 1240px
 * container leaves a third of it empty and that reads as something failing to load. This
 * one is the exception, because the design asks for it and because what follows
 * immediately below is a full-width card that fills the band on its own. The empty right
 * half reads as air rather than as a shortfall only because the card is there to catch it.
 * If the card is ever removed, this hero needs the two-column treatment back.
 */
---

<section class="hero">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow hero__eyebrow">Get in touch</p>

    <h1 class="hero__title">Let&rsquo;s find the friction.</h1>

    <p class="hero__subhead">
      Whether you have a specific workflow in mind or just know something isn&rsquo;t moving
      as fast as it should, we&rsquo;re here to help you find the right place to start with
      AI.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .hero {
    background: var(--ark-surface);
    padding-block: 76px 40px;
  }

  .hero__eyebrow {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
  }

  /* The measure sits on the element carrying the font-size, never on a wrapper — `ch`
   * resolves against the element's own size, so on a wrapper it counts 16px characters
   * and cramps a display heading to a third of its width. */
  .hero__title {
    font-size: var(--ark-h1);
    line-height: var(--ark-lh-h1);
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    max-width: 20ch;
  }

  .hero__subhead {
    margin-top: 26px;
    font-size: var(--ark-body-lg);
    line-height: var(--ark-lh-body);
    color: var(--ark-body-ink);
    max-width: 58ch;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .hero {
      padding-block: 52px 28px;
    }

    .hero__subhead {
      margin-top: 18px;
      max-width: 44ch;
    }
  }

  /* Phone and any portrait screen: the block centres, the text inside it does not.
   * Centred prose is harder to read, which matters more than usual under the
   * plain-language rule — but a left-flush block under a centred headline reads as a
   * misalignment, so the box centres even though its text does not. The auto margin sits
   * in the SAME query that centres the headline so the two can never disagree. */
  @media (max-width: 900px), (orientation: portrait) {
    .hero__title,
    .hero__eyebrow {
      text-align: center;
      margin-inline: auto;
    }

    .hero__subhead {
      margin-inline: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
